{"step_index":284,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T09:26:29Z","thinking":"**Assessing the Feedback**\n\nI'm currently dissecting the feedback regarding the shape-to-letter transformation. The bluntness of \"very, very bad\" is guiding my focus. It seems the core issue is a failure in resemblance; the resulting shapes aren't registering as letters. My priority is to understand *how* to make them more letter-like, per the new requirement.\n\n\n**Reimagining the Assembly**\n\nNow I'm envisioning the letter formation as a puzzle, inspired by toddler toys. The key is a flawless transition. I've considered that the shape pieces, derived from the letter's own silhouette, will assemble perfectly, eliminating the jarring \"replacement\" of the current method. I'm leaning toward creating custom polygons or extracting paths from `SVGMobject` or `Text` objects in Manim, as the basis for the individual puzzle pieces. Alternatively, primitive shapes are an option.\n\n\n**Refining the Shape Design**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a \"block puzzle\" aesthetic for the letters. The focus is on clean, perfectly fitted shapes that, when assembled, *are* the letter, avoiding any visual \"transformation flash\". I'm experimenting with the simplest shapes, like rectangles, to define a base. Considering the \"H\", \"L\", and \"T\" as easy examples. I'm moving away from angled rectangles for the \"A,\" since they present perfect corner challenges, and may opt for a more primitive set of shapes to avoid complexity.\n\n\n**Constructing the Letters**\n\nI'm now fully immersed in building A, B, C, and D using block shapes, going for a \"pixel font\" aesthetic. The \"A\" is straightforward with its four blocks, like LEGO, and the \"B\" is shaping up with its spine and curved elements. \"C\" will be three blocks, and I'm currently defining \"D\" in similar fashion. I'm focusing on simplicity and ensuring they assemble perfectly from their simple consti